-- title:
--   Local Poincaré inequality from a single shared cone direction
-- statement:
--   Let $d \ge 1$, let $v \in \mathbb{R}^d$ be a unit vector, let $0 < \vartheta \le \pi/2$, let $\alpha \ge 0$, let $f : \mathbb{R}^d \to \mathbb{R}$ be measurable, and let $U \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d$ be an arbitrary set. Then
--
--   $$\omega_d \iint_{U \times U} \big(f(t)-f(s)\big)^2\,\|s-t\|^{-d-\alpha}\,d(s,t) \;\le\; C\,\omega_d \int_{U}\!\!\int_{\{z\,:\,z-s \in \tilde V(v,\vartheta)\}} 2\big(f(z)-f(s)\big)^2 \|z-s\|^{-d-\alpha}\,dz\,ds \;+\; C'\,\omega_d \int_{U}\!\!\int_{\{z\,:\,z-t \in \tilde V(v,\vartheta)\}} 2\big(f(t)-f(z)\big)^2\|z-t\|^{-d-\alpha}\,dz\,dt ,$$
--
--   where
--   $$C \;=\; \frac{\bigl(1+\tfrac{3}{\sin\vartheta}\bigr)^{2d+\alpha}}{\bigl(\tfrac{3}{\sin\vartheta}-1\bigr)^{d}} \quad (\texttt{chainConst}), \qquad C' \;=\; \frac{\bigl(2+\tfrac{3}{\sin\vartheta}\bigr)^{2d+\alpha}}{\bigl(\tfrac{3}{\sin\vartheta}-2\bigr)^{d}} \quad (\texttt{chainConst\_prime}),$$
--   $\omega_d = |\bar B(0,1)|$ is the volume of the unit ball of $\mathbb{R}^d$ (`unitBallVol d`), and
--   $$\tilde V(v,\vartheta) \;=\; \Bigl\{h \in \mathbb{R}^d : h \ne 0,\ \cos\vartheta < \tfrac{\langle v,h\rangle}{\lVert h\rVert}\Bigr\} \qquad (\texttt{cone})$$
--   is the one-sided cone with axis $v$ and aperture $\vartheta$.
--
--   The left-hand side is the *full* fractional energy of $f$ over all pairs of $U$ — no pairs are excluded — and the right-hand side involves only differences taken along the single cone direction $v$. So when every point of $U$ shares one admissible cone direction, control of the cone-restricted energy gives control of the whole $H^{\alpha/2}$ energy on $U$, with constants depending only on $d$, $\vartheta$ and $\alpha$. This is the inclusion that discharges the a priori hypothesis of the limiting argument on regions with a common direction.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** $U$ is not assumed measurable, and no integrability is assumed of $f$: everything is a lower Lebesgue integral into `ℝ≥0∞`, so the inequality is trivially true whenever the right-hand side is $+\infty$. The inner integrals on the right are over all of $\mathbb{R}^d$ intersected with the cone translated to the base point, *not* over $U$, so the right-hand side may see values of $f$ outside $U$. The factor $\omega_d$ appears on both sides and is not cancelled.
